<blockquote class="uncited"><div><blockquote class="uncited"><div>The only way to align the frequency calibration is to use a SDR. Transmit on 435MHz FM on your other radio , and zoom in using your SDR to look at the centre frequency of the transmission.  <br>When you have centered your SDR display on the middle of the freq, with the SDR zoomed in as far as it will go. Then go into the calibration screen of the OpenGD77 radio, and in the UHF / 435MHz freq cal page, adjust the freq until the transmission is exactly centred where the other radio was transmitting.<br><br>If you don't have an SDR dongle, borrow one from your local club, or buy one, they are quite cheap and the PC software is free</div></blockquote>If one have a very precise freq counter (12 digits - locked to a 10MHz GPSDO) would that do ?<br><br>I mean calibrate all radios to  435MHz FM against the counter. And then adjust the "PI HOTSPOT" last to match the calibrated radios ?</div></blockquote>I use my counter to calibrate all my radios and for hotspots I start the pistar-mmdvmcal in carrier mode and adjust the frequency.<br>The offset value is then added in the mmdvm setttings.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=2867">SA0BUX</a> — Fri Dec 06, 2024 6:20 am</p><hr />
