<blockquote class="uncited"><div>any suggestion to solve this problem?</div></blockquote>OK, here is the dirty way of making it work, hopefully.<br><br>First watch this video. It is how to use the calibration on the Pi-Star hotspot.<br><br><a href="https://youtu.be/n81MCz4VcSs?si=CpREL1KOzTfX7uH9" class="postlink">https://youtu.be/n81MCz4VcSs?si=CpREL1KOzTfX7uH9</a><br><br>Now, because you have two radio. you will have to find a compromise, a sweet spot so to speak.<br>We know the hotspot work with your old radio, so we will start calibration with your new radio.<br><br>calibrate to the best with your new radio, then take note of the offset number. Write it down, but don't click save.<br><br>Now Calibrate using your old radio, Take note of the offset. <br><br>Then use the offset in the middle. For example if the offset for new radio is +100 and the offset for the old radio is -50. Then the middle spot is +25. click save. and stop.<br><br>Now hope fully the hotspot will work with bout of your radio.<br><br>Hope this help.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=5068">KA1CM</a> — Tue Dec 03, 2024 10:48 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
